INT. SALT MINE TUNNEL - FORWARD LISTENING POST - NIGHT, 1916

White walls glitter under oil lamps. The passage narrows to a crawl at one end, where a pickaxe lies abandoned. Everything tastes of brine and iron. A telephone hangs on a nail, its wire running up into the dark.

SGT. HIRAM STALLWORTH, 40s, stands against the wall, thumb moving over a rosary he doesn't believe in. CPL. OTTILIE BRANDT, 30s, medic, kneels over a canvas bag, counting bandages. PVT. EMEKA OGBONNA, 19, crouches at the narrowing, head cocked, listening. PVT. SAMUEL "SOOT" BEAUMONT, 22, sits on an ammunition crate sharpening a bayonet.

Silence. A drip from the roof. Then, from far above, a faint rhythm of thumps.

EMEKA
They're digging again.

HIRAM
How close?

EMEKA
Same as an hour ago.

SOOT
That's not close or far. That's just there.

OTTILIE
Soot, hold still. Your hands are shaking.

SOOT
They are not.

OTTILIE
Then it's the crate.

HIRAM
Brandt, enough.

OTTILIE
Sergeant.

The thumps stop. All four look up. A dry rain of salt falls from the ceiling and tings on the helmet at Emeka's feet.

EMEKA
(whisper)
Did you hear that?

SOOT
Hear what?

EMEKA
Nothing. That's what I'm saying.

HIRAM
Stand to.

They move. Ottilie closes her bag. Soot slides the bayonet home. Emeka backs from the narrowing, rifle level. Hiram unhooks the telephone and puts it to his ear. Nothing. He taps the cradle. Nothing.

HIRAM (CONT'D)
Line's dead.

SOOT
Course it is.

OTTILIE
Don't.

SOOT
I'm only saying.

OTTILIE
Don't.

A single drop of water hits the floor. Another. They all watch it. The echo seems to travel for a long time.

EMEKA
I used to hear my mother singing in the pit at home.

HIRAM
Did you?

EMEKA
Same note. Over and over.

OTTILIE
What was the note?

EMEKA
I don't know. I never had a name for it.

Soot laughs, not unkindly. Hiram almost smiles. The thumps resume overhead, three, then four, then a pause.

HIRAM
We hold.

SOOT
Till when?

HIRAM
Till we're told.

OTTILIE
Told by who?

HIRAM
(quietly)
That's not for us to ask.

He hangs up the phone. Folds his arms. The four of them settle in the white tunnel, listening to the mountain breathe.
